{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Your garment did not wear out from upon you.</b> Behold, this was a great miracle in the alteration of the nature of the world, for garments wear out through constant wearing by way of nature, but here, in the case of the wilderness generation, they remained constantly renewed, and time and sweat had no dominion over them to consume them." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> Rabbi Eliezer asked Rabbi Shimon: From where did Israel have clothing for all forty years? He said to him: From that which the ministering angels clothed them, as it is said (Ezekiel 16:10): ״ואלבישך רקמה״. What is רקמה? Rabbi Simai says: porphyra. He said to him: And did they not wear out? He said: And did you not read, ״שמלתך לא בלתה מעליך״ — 'your garment did not wear out from upon you'? He said to him: And did the small children grow along with them? He said: Go and learn from this lizard, whose garment grows with it. He said to him: And did they not need laundering? He said: The cloud would scour them and press them. He said to him: And were they not scorched? He said: Go and learn from these סיטין, which are only pressed by fire. And did they produce mildew? He said: In their death they did not; in their lifetime all the more so! He said to him: And did they not become foul from the smell of sweat when they did not change their garments? He said: The well would bring up for them varieties of herbs whose fragrance spread from one end of the world to the other, and they would wave them, as it is said (Psalms 23:2): ״בנאות דשא ירביצני״ וגו׳. Solomon, peace be upon him, came and explained (Song of Songs 4:11): ״וריח שלמותיך כריח לבנון״. Up to here is in the Midrash on Psalms, in the psalm 'HaShem is my shepherd, I shall not want.' And the meaning of סיטין is garments; and a garment the measure of a span is called a סיט." "<b>And in the chapters of</b> Rabbi Eliezer: סיט כפול, and its meaning is a span. And in the Name of 72, when He wishes to demonstrate His divinity and His might, He stretches out His hand against idolaters and removes them from the world. And understand this." "<b>And your foot did not swell.</b> It did not become bloated — from the word בצק (dough) — for thus is the way of those who walk on the road, that their feet become swollen." "<b>Or</b> one may say this refers to the Levites, who carried the Ark in the wilderness on their shoulders barefoot. And even though the law of the priests is to carry the Ark, and this is a matter entrusted to them and not to the Levites, the priests were at that time in the wilderness few in number, until they multiplied and took up what was theirs." "<b>And you should know</b> Onkelos on this: that the shoes on their feet did not wear out."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {2 "The phrase 'בשם של ע״ב' (in the Name of 72) refers to the 72-letter divine Name; 'עכו״ם' read literally as 'idolaters' (עובדי כוכבים ומזלות); the passage transitions abruptly from the lexicographical note to a Kabbalistic remark, preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:51.978419156Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}